---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Contacto from '../../components/Contacto.astro';
import Pendiente from '../../components/Pendiente.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/paths';
import { site, linkedinPending } from '../../config';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const paths = { es: 'perfil/', en: 'en/profile/' };

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Person',
  name: site.name,
  url: new URL(withBase('en/profile/'), Astro.site).href,
  email: `mailto:${site.email}`,
  jobTitle: 'Product Owner and Delivery Lead',
  description:
    'Product Owner and Delivery Lead. Also designs and builds websites for local businesses.',
  ...(linkedinPending ? {} : { sameAs: [site.linkedin, site.repo] }),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  paths={paths}
  title="Profile — Francisco López"
  description="Product Owner and Delivery Lead: how I work, what I work on, and why that matters when what you want is a small website."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="wrap pt-10 md:pt-16">
    <h1>Delivery is the trade.</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-[34em] text-[1.2rem]">
      My day job is running software projects: deciding what gets built first, what stays
      out, and making sure what was promised is there on the date it was promised.
      Building sites for small businesses comes out of that, not the other way round.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap prosa mt-16 md:mt-20">
    <h2>Where I've been</h2>
    <Pendiente>
      Pending: real years, companies and sectors. This section is written as a frame —
      send me the details and I'll finish it.
    </Pendiente>
    <p>
      Product Owner and Delivery Lead on product teams: taking what the business needs,
      turning it into something buildable, and holding the delivery together until it's in
      the hands of the people who use it.
    </p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>
    <p>
      Most projects don't go wrong for a technical reason. They go wrong because nobody
      said out loud what was not going to be built. That's where I start: what's in,
      what's out, and what happens if something falls over.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then short stretches, with something finished at the end of each one. I'd rather
      show one working piece than describe the whole thing in a deck. When there's bad
      news, I give it early: a date that moves with notice is an adjustment, and the same
      date moved the day before is somebody else's problem.
    </p>
    <p>
      And I don't leave hidden debt behind. Not on a software project and not on a bar's
      website: if something was done quickly and will need revisiting, I say so before we
      close.
    </p>

    <h2>Why I'm telling you this</h2>
    <p>
      If all you need is a page, none of this should matter much. It matters in one way:
      that the work actually gets delivered. A small job is exactly where you notice
      somebody who can't finish — it drags, it stalls halfway, and you end up with half a
      website and nobody to chase.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also take freelance Product Owner and Delivery Lead work. It's set out under
      <a href={withBase('en/services/')}>services</a>.
    </p>

    {
      !linkedinPending && (
        <p>
          My full background is on <a href={site.linkedin}>LinkedIn</a>.
        </p>
      )
    }
    {linkedinPending && <Pendiente>Pending: LinkedIn link (src/config.ts).</Pendiente>}
  </section>

  <Contacto lang={lang} />
</Base>
